---
import { SITE, withBase } from '../games/registry.ts';
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';

const UPDATED = '2026-10-09';
---

<Base title="Privacy" description="What Whetstone stores (only preferences, saved tools and a card cache in your own browser) and which requests leave this site.">
  <article class="container privacy">
    <header class="privacy__head">
      <p class="kicker">Privacy</p>
      <h1>Nothing to track.</h1>
      <p class="privacy__lead">
        No accounts, no cookies, no analytics, no ads, no fingerprinting. The site is a set of static files; it has no server
        of its own that could receive your data.
      </p>
      <p class="muted">Last updated <time datetime={UPDATED}>{UPDATED}</time>.</p>
    </header>

    <div class="prose">
      <h2>Stored in your browser</h2>
      <p>
        A few preferences and the things you save in the tools are kept in your browser's local storage so they survive a
        reload. They never leave your device, and clearing this site's data in your browser removes them. Keys start with
        <code>whetstone:</code>:
      </p>
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th scope="col">Key</th>
            <th scope="col">What it holds</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td><code>whetstone:site:theme</code></td>
            <td>Your light or dark theme choice.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><code>whetstone:site:period:*</code></td>
            <td>The time period you last picked on stats pages.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><code>whetstone:hots:*</code></td>
            <td>
              Heroes of the Storm: your hero pool and favourites (<code>pool</code>), the hero grid's dimming choice, the
              draft tool's sort mode and "suggest from" choice, and the game mode you last picked on stats pages.
            </td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><code>whetstone:mtga:*</code></td>
            <td>MTG Arena: meta decklists you saved, your meta comparison choice and the wildcards you own.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><code>whetstone:pokemon:*</code></td>
            <td>Pokémon Champions: teams you saved and the team currently open in the builder (the team preview reads it too).</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
      <p>
        <strong>Card data cache.</strong> The MTG Arena tools keep a copy of the public card index they analyse with in an
        IndexedDB database named <code>whetstone-mtga</code> (one record per card pool, replaced when the card data is
        updated), so the multi-megabyte download happens once. It holds no information about you.
      </p>

      <h2>Offline copy</h2>
      <p>
        A service worker keeps a copy of the site's scripts, styles and icons, plus the pages, data files, images and tool
        workers you have opened, so tools keep working on a flaky connection. It lives in your browser's cache storage (caches
        whose names start with <code>whetstone</code>) and is removed with the rest of the site data.
      </p>

      <h2>Share links</h2>
      <p>
        Drafts, decks and teams you share are encoded after the <code>#</code> in the link. Browsers don't send that part to
        any server; it only means something to the person who opens the link.
      </p>

      <h2>Requests to other sites</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>Hosting.</strong> Pages are served by GitHub Pages. GitHub may log technical data such as IP addresses for
          security and operations, as described in the
          <a href="https://docs.github.com/en/site-policy/privacy-policies/github-general-privacy-statement" rel="noopener">GitHub General Privacy Statement</a>.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>MTG Arena card images and lookups.</strong> MTG Arena pages load card images from Scryfall's image servers
          (<code>cards.scryfall.io</code>). If you tick the option to look up card names that aren't in the built-in index,
          only those names are sent to Scryfall's API (<code>api.scryfall.com</code>). Those requests go straight from your
          browser to Scryfall, under <a href="https://scryfall.com/docs/privacy" rel="noopener">Scryfall's privacy policy</a>.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Videos and streams, only on click.</strong> Heroes of the Storm guide videos and live streams show a plain
          card first. Nothing is requested from YouTube or Twitch until you press <em>Play here</em>; the player then loads
          from <code>www.youtube-nocookie.com</code> or <code>player.twitch.tv</code> under their own privacy policies.
          <em>Open on YouTube</em> and <em>Open on Twitch</em> are ordinary links.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Everything else</strong> (data files, hero portraits, ability icons, Pokémon sprites, scripts and styles) is
          served from this site. Outbound links to guides and sources take you to those sites, under their own policies.
        </li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Questions</h2>
      <p>
        Ask in a <a href={SITE.feedback} rel="noopener">GitHub issue</a> or read the <a href={SITE.repo} rel="noopener">source
        code</a> to check any of the above. See also <a href={withBase('about/')}>About</a> and
        <a href={withBase('credits/')}>Credits</a>.
      </p>
    </div>
  </article>
</Base>

<style>
  .privacy {
    display: grid;
    gap: var(--sp-6);
    padding-block: var(--sp-7) var(--sp-8);
  }

  .privacy__head {
    display: grid;
    gap: var(--sp-3);
    max-width: 48rem;
  }

  .privacy__lead {
    color: var(--text-muted);
    font-size: var(--fs-lg);
  }

  .prose table {
    font-size: var(--fs-sm);
  }

  .prose td:first-child {
    white-space: nowrap;
  }

  @media (max-width: 30rem) {
    .prose td:first-child {
      white-space: normal;
    }
  }
</style>
